Original commit body from 297d4008d9326f896fac6d39d922d4b9f69444e6:

A cached survey answers sketch, routes, verify-claims and every other
command that reads a behavior map, but its key (repo state, backend set,
trace_deps, analyzer identity) did not include the user catalogue channels
the analysis reads. A frameworks.d / library_signatures.d /
dataflow_patterns.d file added, edited or broken after a survey was cached
was never read: sketch kept exiting 0 on a file survey refused at exit 2.
config.toml and .hypergumbo.toml had the same gap (neither is source the
state hash covers), so a [merge] edit or a broken config was masked too.

Fix:
- yaml_catalogs.CatalogSpec gains channel_stage (analysis | command),
  required for every family with a user channel and refused without one
  by validate_registry. frameworks, dataflow_patterns and
  library_signatures are analysis-stage; io_primitives, the taint
  families and function_summaries are read per command over the cached
  graph and stay out of the key.
- catalogue_home.analysis_channel_token digests every file in the
  analysis-stage channels (channel-relative name + sha256 of bytes,
  sorted); user_config.config_cache_token digests both config tiers.
  _get_results_cache_dir appends -cat-<hash> / -cfg-<hash>, each absent
  when its files are, so users with neither keep every existing key.
- A behavioural test spies on user_channel_files during a real survey
  and asserts the families read equal the registry's analysis-stage set,
  so a loader that starts reading a command-stage channel fails it.

cache-status needs no change: it counts state directories and does not
describe the key's components (it did not explain -deps-* either).

